**1. A computer-aided method for visually presenting electronic raw datasets grouped by theme, the method comprising:**

receiving, by one or more processors, a plurality of electronic raw datasets, each electronic raw dataset comprising unstructured, semi-structured, or structured data items;

extracting, by the one or more processors, from each electronic raw dataset a plurality of properties representative of content or metadata of the respective electronic raw dataset;

generating, by the one or more processors, for each electronic raw dataset a corresponding property vector based on the extracted plurality of properties;

processing, by the one or more processors, the property vectors using at least one of a clustering algorithm, a classification algorithm, or a summarization algorithm to determine a thematic grouping for each electronic raw dataset;

reducing, by the one or more processors, a dimensionality of the property vectors to generate, for each electronic raw dataset, a two-dimensional coordinate representation in a visual presentation space;

assigning, by the one or more processors, to each electronic raw dataset a third coordinate dimension comprising at least one of a time value associated with the electronic raw dataset or an identifier associated with the electronic raw dataset;

generating, by the one or more processors, a three-dimensional visual representation in which each electronic raw dataset is represented at a position defined by the two-dimensional coordinate representation and the third coordinate dimension; and

displaying, on a display device, the three-dimensional visual representation such that electronic raw datasets having related thematic groupings are visually presented in proximity to one another or with a common visual attribute.

1. A computer-implemented method for three-dimensional visualization of raw data sets, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, a plurality of raw data sets, each raw data set of the plurality of raw data sets being associated with a respective timestamp or unique identifier;

computing, by the one or more processors, for each raw data set, at least one property matrix representing one or more properties of the respective raw data set;

performing, by the one or more processors, one or more analytic operations on the at least one property matrix for each raw data set to generate analytic results corresponding to the respective raw data set;

reducing, by the one or more processors, the analytic results corresponding to each raw data set to a two-dimensional representation defining first and second visualization coordinates; and

generating, by the one or more processors, a three-dimensional visualization in which the first and second visualization coordinates define two spatial dimensions and the respective timestamp or unique identifier associated with each raw data set defines a third spatial dimension.

1. A computer-implemented method for visualising relationships in a plurality of electronic datasets, the method comprising:

   receiving, by one or more processors, a plurality of electronic datasets;

   transforming each of the electronic datasets into a respective feature vector comprising a plurality of numerical feature values;

   assembling the feature vectors into a data matrix;

   analysing the data matrix to determine at least one of clusters, associations, classifications and statistical summaries of the electronic datasets;

   generating, from the data matrix or from a result of the analysing, a two-dimensional mapping in which each electronic dataset is represented by a respective point having first and second coordinates;

   assigning to each respective point a third coordinate based on one of a time value associated with the corresponding electronic dataset and a unique identifier of the corresponding electronic dataset; and

   displaying, on a display device, a three-dimensional representation of the plurality of electronic datasets, wherein the first and second coordinates define a position of each respective point in a two-dimensional plane and the third coordinate defines a position of each respective point along a z-axis.
